    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Three ways to say a consonant",
     "texto": [
      "In Korean there are **plain**, **aspirated** and **tense** consonants, and they change the meaning.",
      "The **aspirated** consonants (ㅋ, ㅌ, ㅍ, ㅊ) are said with a **puff of air**, like the «k» in «key». Notice: they are the plain ones with one extra stroke.",
      "The **tense** consonants (ㄲ, ㄸ, ㅃ, ㅆ, ㅉ) are written double and are said **dry and strong**, with no air at all. They are very similar to the «k», «t» and «p» in «sky», «stop» and «spin», said with energy.",
      "In the pronunciation guides you will see the tense consonants written with a double letter: kk, tt, pp, ss, jj."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
